    "text_delta": "e or biased questioning during the interview, deviations from the hospital's formal scoring rubric, or documented disparate treatment. The panel’s demographic composition alone does not meet that bar.\n* Making an issue of the panel's gender without hard evidence would likely be perceived as an inability to accept a competitive loss and could severely damage your professional reputation within hospital networks, which tend to be tightly interconnected.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a Polite Follow-Up:** \n   Wait until early next week, then send a brief, courteous email to HR or the panel chair:\n   > *\"I am following up on my request from two weeks ago regarding written feedback on my interviews for the Head of Clinical Laboratory position. I greatly value the opportunity I had to interview, and constructive feedback would be immensely helpful for my ongoing professional development.\"*\n2. **Focus on Growth Areas:**\n   When you do receive feedback, look closely at where the scoring differed—specifically regarding strategic vision, budgetary experience, and leadership style.\n3. **Reflect on the Interview Exchanges:**\n   Were there specific moments where the discussion felt misaligned, or where you felt your operational leadership scope wasn't fully conveyed? That reflection will be far more beneficial for your next leadership application than focusing on panel demographics.\n",
